    (0, None, '\'Well, let\'s try your famous signature sauce.\' "Nu-ka" is a nudge, like \'come on then\'. "Firmennyy", \'signature\', comes from firma, a company, so it means the house\'s own.'),
    (1, None, '\'This sauce is true high culinary art.\' "Iskusstvo", art, is neuter, so nastoyashcheye, vysokoye and kulinarnoye all end in -oye. The dash in writing is where English would put \'is\'.'),
    (2, None, '\'Oh, the fresh bread fell on the floor.\' "Upal" ends in plain -l because khleb, bread, is masculine. Na pol is \'onto the floor\'; lying on the floor would be na polu.'),
    (3, None, '\'Right now we will quickly bring you new bread.\' "Prinesyom" joins pri-, arrival, to nesti, to carry. Tebe, \'to you\', is the ty form; Barinov and the owner are old friends.'),
    (4, None, '\'No, I am in a big hurry for an important meeting.\' "Speshu", \'I hurry\', is the everyday way to say you\'re in a rush. "Vstrecha", a meeting, comes from vstrechat\', to meet, and after na it becomes vstrechu.'),
    (5, None, '\'But you must taste this rare masterpiece!\' "Rasprobovat\'" adds ras- to probovat\', so it means tasting until you really get the flavour. "Ty obyazan", \'you are obliged\', is much stronger than plain \'must\'.'),
    (6, None, '\'I will return at exactly seven o\'clock tonight.\' V sem\' chasov is \'at seven hours\', the clock pattern again. "Vechera", \'of the evening\', marks p.m.'),
    (7, None, '\'We will prepare the very best sauce for you.\' "Samyy luchshiy" is \'the very best\', with samyy for emphasis. "Dlya", \'for\', puts ty into the form tebya: dlya tebya.'),
    (8, None, '\'Agreed, Viktor, do not let me down tonight.\' "Dogovorilis\'" is \'we\'ve agreed\', the standard way to close a deal. Ne podvedite, \'don\'t let me down\', ends in -te because it\'s aimed at Viktor and his whole kitchen.'),
    (9, None, '\'The Chef never deceives his favorite guests.\' "Svoikh", \'his own\', points back to the subject, the chef. "Lyubimyy", favourite, is related to lyubit\', to love.'),
    (10, None, "'Until tonight then, I wish you great work.' Do vechera, 'until the evening', is a friendly 'see you tonight'. After zhelayu, 'I wish', the wish takes 'of' endings: otlichnoy raboty."),
